the camp for displaced people in mithush  for half a year it has been home for  julieta carlos like everyone here  she fled her village in the north after  attacks from a group  people here call al-shabab when  i was on the way to my field and saw men  decapitating my neighbors  i started running immediately and looked  for a place to hide in the bushes  after a long time i went back to my  house but they set it on fire  all my belongings were burned  hears terrifying stories like that every  day  he's the only psychologist in the camp  working in five others as well  talking is part of the healing process  it is not only medicine that can help  talking sometimes crying can help too if  people need to cry they should  it helps them to heal to take out what  makes them suffer  for many here trauma comes second when  you have to fight for survival every day  julieta carlos says her family only  received rice and grains from the  government  forcing them to collect grass in the  bush which they cook and eat  eight people are living inside this tiny  hut  and julieta carlos just told us that it  is raining season now and sometimes when  it's raining in the night  there's no floor so they can't lie down  they have to try and stand inside this  tiny little hut and this is not only the  situation for her family more than 6 000  people are living in this camp  under terrible conditions  there are outbreaks of cholera a lack of  basic medications  and food being confined to the camp with  little to do  many are still scared  [Music]  the group counselling sessions provide  an outlet for these displaced people  to voice their problems but ultimately  what  almost everyone here wants is to return  home  in peace  [Music]  today the rights group amnesty  international released a report on the  islamist insurgency in northern  mozambique the report is based on  interviews with internally  displaced people from the communities  that have been impacted  by the increased fighting in the region  now amnesty international's david  martinez joins me to tell us more  about that report hi david what does  your report say about the raging  insurgency in northern mozambique it's  displaced thousands of people  some of whom we just saw in our report  people of cabdelgad are caught in a  triangle of violence  war crimes and human rights abuses  because on the one hand  the group fighters are shooting and  beheading civilians  burning their homes looting their food  and property  abducting women and girls subjecting  them to sexual  physical and emotional violence  including forced marriages  secondly we have government security  forces  who are subjecting detainees to torture  and extrajudicial killings  and lastly the south african private  military company  big advisory group that was hired by the  mozambican government to take part in  this conflict  this company has been using machine guns  and hand grenades from helicopters  so indiscriminately without  distinguishing between  civilians and military targets in a  nutshell  that's the summary of the report david  amnesty international  um accuses government security forces  uh of committing war crimes uh tell us  more about that  there are those uh fighters of the armed  group  who are detained and captured some of  them wounded  and they are often subjected  to torture and extrajudicial killings  and you have civilians as well in those  neighborhoods in the armed conflict area  who are also uh tortured  and you know killed some of them  possibly disappeared  and never to be heard from them again  now  the most american government must  promptly open independent  impartial and transparent investigations  into these  credible allegations as well as others  that are not included in our report  and bring all those responsible to  justice  they must also give a humanitarian  agency  to access to capital guards so that they  can do their work  providing aid to people in need in  capital guard  all right that's david martinez of  amnesty international speaking to us  there thank you david
 

